\section*{Simplex maximum for projection-body volume}

\subsection{OpenAI's claim}
OpenAI presents the following claims relevant to this question, with manuscript numbers following paper order in Sources. In manuscript 1 (family 088; 2026-09-24), the asserted result is that taking the Cartesian product of two dimension-ten simplices yields a body whose normalized projection volume exceeds the simplex value in dimension twenty, refuting the proposed maximum.

\subsection{Mathematical statement of the claimed result}
\paragraph{Manuscript 1.}
For a $d$-dimensional convex body, define its projection body by $h_{\Pi K}(u)=\operatorname{vol}_{d-1}(\operatorname{proj}_{u^{\perp}}K)$ on unit vectors, and set $R_d(K)=|\Pi K|/|K|^{d-1}$ and $c_d=(d+1)d^d/d!$. Take the standard simplex $T_{10}=\operatorname{conv}(0,e_1,\ldots,e_{10})$ and the product $K=T_{10}\times T_{10}\subset\mathbb R^{20}$. The exact asserted comparison is
\[
 \frac{R_{20}(K)}{c_{20}}=\frac{121\binom{20}{10}}{21\cdot2^{20}}
 =\frac{22\,355\,476}{22\,020\,096}>1.
\]
Thus a simplex does not maximize projection-body volume normalized by $|K|^{d-1}$ in every dimension.

\subsection{Outline of the argument}
For manuscript 1 (family 088; 2026-09-24), facet-vector formulas give an exact projection-body volume for a simplex and its Cartesian products. Comparing the resulting finite quantities produces a ratio strictly above one; the product formula also explains the large-dimensional excess.

\subsection{Scope relative to this problem}
The displayed question has a negative answer under the manuscript's stated construction. Its counterexample meets the conditions in this problem and defeats the universal assertion, so this is a full disproof claim. The supporting result is manuscript 1 (family 088; 2026-09-24).

\subsection{Formal verification}
For manuscript 1 (family 088; 2026-09-24), OpenAI lists Lean statements for this assertion: taking the Cartesian product of two dimension-ten simplices yields a body whose normalized projection volume exceeds the simplex value in dimension twenty, refuting the proposed maximum.

The central assertion is within the documented formal scope; the additional applications retain their own scope limits.

The linked family Comparator checks are \texttt{PettyProjectionVolume.lean}, \texttt{ProjectionCounterexample.lean}, \texttt{ProjectionVolume.lean}.

Their named declarations include \texttt{OAI.Paper092.product\_counterexample}, \texttt{OAI.PettyProjection.petty\_projection\_volume}, \texttt{OAI.ProjectionCounterexample.universal\_simplex\_upper\_bound\_false}.

This site has not executed the Lean code. Described statement coverage is not an independent proof check.

\subsection{Status caveat}
The manuscripts were produced by an internal OpenAI model. OpenAI cautions that results without formalization can contain mistakes. This site has not independently checked the proofs; the label records the scope of OpenAI's claim.

